# WhatsApp Clone – Cursor / AntiGravity Rules
# (WebRTC Voice & Video Calling)

## GENERAL PRINCIPLES
- Follow clean architecture and strict separation of concerns
- Optimize for real-time scalability (millions of concurrent users)
- No hardcoded values (IDs, roles, URLs, STUN/TURN servers, secrets)
- Prefer explicit, readable, maintainable code over shortcuts
- Assume enterprise-level production standards
- Design for horizontal scaling and event-driven systems

---

## BACKEND – SPRING BOOT RULES

### Architecture
- Use layered architecture:
  controller → service → domain → repository
- Controllers must not contain business logic
- Services must not depend on controllers
- Use DTOs; never expose entities directly
- Each module must be isolated
- WebRTC signaling must be isolated from REST APIs

### Package Structure
com.whatsappclone
 ├── auth
 ├── security
 ├── user
 ├── contact
 ├── chat
 │    ├── message
 │    ├── conversation
 │    └── attachment
 ├── voicecall
 │    ├── signaling
 │    ├── session
 │    └── webrtc
 ├── videocall
 │    ├── signaling
 │    ├── session
 │    └── webrtc
 ├── presence
 ├── notification
 ├── dashboard
 ├── exception
 └── config

---

## AUTHENTICATION & AUTHORIZATION

- JWT-based authentication only
- Stateless session management
- BCrypt password hashing
- Token must include:
  - userId
  - phoneNumber
- Only USER role exists currently
- Design role handling to support future roles (ADMIN, MODERATOR)
- Secure all APIs except:
  - Login

---

## REAL-TIME COMMUNICATION RULES

### Signaling
- WebSocket-based signaling mandatory
- REST APIs must not be used for call signaling
- Event-driven communication only
- Backend is the source of truth for call state

### Presence
- Presence must be backend-managed
- Presence states:
  - ONLINE
  - OFFLINE
  - IN_CALL
- Presence data must be ephemeral (Redis / in-memory store)
- Frontend must never be the source of truth

---

## WEBRTC – VOICE & VIDEO CALL RULES

### Core Rules

- Backend acts strictly as signaling server
- Media must flow peer-to-peer only
- No audio/video streams through backend
- STUN/TURN servers must be environment-configured
- ICE server rotation must be supported
- WebRTC configuration must be extensible for SFU support

### Voice Call Module

- Voice calls must be implemented as a dedicated module
- Call types:
  - AUDIO
- Each voice call must include:
  - voice_call_session_id
  - caller_id
  - receiver_id OR group_id
  - call_status (INITIATED / RINGING / CONNECTED / ENDED)
- Handle:
  - Missed calls
  - Rejected calls
  - Network interruptions
  - Reconnection attempts
- Voice call state must be persisted backend-side
- No call state may exist only on frontend

### Voice Call Module


- Video calls must be implemented as a dedicated module
- Call types:
  - VIDEO
- Each video call must include:
  - video_call_session_id
  - caller_id
  - receiver_id OR group_id
  - call_status (INITIATED / RINGING / CONNECTED / ENDED)
- Handle:
  - Camera/microphone permission failures
  - Network drops
  - Reconnect attempts
- Video call state must be persisted backend-side
- Group video calls must be SFU-ready (future scalability)
- Screen sharing must be designed as an extension, not a hack

---

## DATABASE RULES (POSTGRESQL)

- Use snake_case for all tables and columns
- All tables must include:
  - id
  - is_active
  - created_at
  - updated_at
- Soft delete preferred over hard delete
- Foreign keys are mandatory
- Use Flyway or Liquibase for migrations

### Core Tables
- users
- contacts
- conversations
- messages
- message_receipts
- voice_call_sessions
- voice_call_participants
- video_call_sessions
- video_call_participants

---

## FRONTEND – REACT + TYPESCRIPT RULES

### Structure
src/
 ├── pages
 ├── components
 ├── services
 ├── hooks
 ├── context
 ├── store
 ├── types
 └── utils

### Rules
- Strict TypeScript (no `any`)
- No API calls directly in components
- Use services layer for API interaction
- Route guards mandatory
- JWT-based auth handling
- Auto logout on token expiry
- WebRTC logic must not live inside UI components
- WebRTC logic must live inside dedicated hooks/services

---

## API DESIGN RULES

- RESTful conventions
- Proper HTTP status codes
- Standard response format:
  - success
  - data
  - error
- Validation errors must be meaningful
- Real-time events must never be mixed with REST responses

---

## ERROR HANDLING & LOGGING

- Centralized exception handling
- No stack traces exposed to frontend
- Structured logging
- Correlation IDs for real-time events
- Graceful frontend error handling

---

## AI BEHAVIOR RULES

- Do not invent requirements
- Do not skip validations
- Ask for clarification if logic is unclear
- Follow existing folder structure
- Prefer extensibility over shortcuts
- Write maintainable, team-friendly, production-ready code
